INT. COIN LAUNDROMAT, BACK BENCH - NIGHT

A narrow laundromat. Dryers turn in rows. Four people, one bench.

JUNE KOWALCZYK-ABARA (20s, hoodie, library book) reads. Next to her, ELIAS TORRES-NAKAMURA (20s, scrubs, tired) folds a towel into a perfect square. Across the aisle, MRS. DELPHINE OYEBANJI (70s, headscarf) watches them both. Beside her, her grandson, KOFI (10, tablet), pretends not to.

Elias drops a sock. It lands on June's shoe. She lifts the sock between two fingers and holds it out without looking up.

ELIAS
Thanks.

JUNE
It's got a hole.

ELIAS
It's on purpose.

She smiles at the book. Neither looks at the other.

MRS. OYEBANJI
(to the room)
Does anybody have change for a five?

ELIAS
No, ma'am.

JUNE
I'll check.

She digs in her bag and comes up with four quarters and a button. Elias stares at the button. She hands over the quarters. Her hand stays where his can reach it. His does, briefly.

KOFI
(to Mrs. Oyebanji, loud)
Grandma, he's holding her hand.

MRS. OYEBANJI
Mind your game.

Elias pulls back. June turns a page she has not read. The dryer buzzes, and all four look at it as if it had spoken.

ELIAS
(muttered)
Mine.

He doesn't get up. June stands, opens the dryer, and hands him his warm laundry without a word. He takes it as if it were something far more breakable.
